1
00:00:16,237 --> 00:00:21,446
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I had a question about upadāna, often translated as assumption.

2
00:00:25,012 --> 00:00:25,273
[SPEAKER_02]: The

3
00:00:26,542 --> 00:00:33,405
[SPEAKER_02]: The whole idea behind the kind of translation of assumption is that it is something that you're not really even aware of.

4
00:00:34,166 --> 00:00:38,308
[SPEAKER_02]: So, somebody who doesn't know they're adopted just assumes that they're not adopted.

5
00:00:38,428 --> 00:00:38,588
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

6
00:00:38,768 --> 00:00:45,791
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not even on their radar as a view they hold until they're confronted with something that indicates otherwise.

7
00:00:46,011 --> 00:00:46,232
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

8
00:00:46,752 --> 00:00:52,875
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how could you possibly get to the point where you are beyond assumptions, which is

9
00:00:53,463 --> 00:00:54,464
[SPEAKER_02]: The state of the arahant.

10
00:00:54,564 --> 00:00:57,726
[SPEAKER_02]: How can you know that there's nothing that you're assuming anymore?

11
00:00:58,187 --> 00:01:01,169
[SPEAKER_02]: It seems like an impossible state.

12
00:01:07,934 --> 00:01:21,685
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it would seem impossible if... that's actually a common thing, that the truth is thought of as a... some sort of external... on some sort of external level, like...

13
00:01:24,183 --> 00:01:33,672
[SPEAKER_00]: Practicing the dhamma to find out the truth, meaning some sort of metaphysical principle that underlies the universe or something.

14
00:01:33,812 --> 00:01:38,496
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, on that level, yes, you will never be sure that you understood everything.

15
00:01:39,557 --> 00:01:40,457
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's...

16
00:01:40,477 --> 00:01:45,021
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, technically, the dhamma is not really...

17
00:01:46,778 --> 00:01:58,163
[SPEAKER_00]: At least not in the sense that people think of reality, like finding out the truth of reality, because, yeah, that notion of reality is really not

18
00:01:59,287 --> 00:02:00,567
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the Buddha was talking about.

19
00:02:00,607 --> 00:02:01,648
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not the five aggregates.

20
00:02:01,728 --> 00:02:12,451
[SPEAKER_00]: It's some external assumption, two or five aggregates of some kind of a external principle that underlies other people and that you can be aware of independently of your point of view.

21
00:02:13,291 --> 00:02:27,015
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you would know that you're free from assumption, first of all, by understanding the level where assumptions are, which is in regard to your own relationship with

22
00:02:27,995 --> 00:02:37,024
[SPEAKER_00]: Any ideas about the truth of reality, for example, like, assumptions would happen in regard to you thinking about this.

23
00:02:37,084 --> 00:02:45,091
[SPEAKER_00]: While you're thinking about what the truth of reality is, philosophizing about that stuff, there would be assumptions there, on that internal level that matters.

24
00:02:45,752 --> 00:02:48,815
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the most fundamental assumption?

25
00:02:49,941 --> 00:02:53,542
[SPEAKER_00]: That this is to talk about, for example.

26
00:02:54,343 --> 00:02:56,403
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's another thing.

27
00:02:56,423 --> 00:02:58,384
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like assumption, assumption in general.

28
00:02:58,864 --> 00:03:01,665
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha said there are four types of assumptions.

29
00:03:02,026 --> 00:03:04,126
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not apart from that, so...

30
00:03:04,546 --> 00:03:04,707
[SPEAKER_02]: Right.

31
00:03:04,987 --> 00:03:08,148
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, like, to kind of use that example again of the adopted.

32
00:03:08,188 --> 00:03:11,469
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like when you become an arahant, you, like, find out you're actually adopted.

33
00:03:12,049 --> 00:03:14,830
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, it's not on that level of things.

34
00:03:15,070 --> 00:03:16,251
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

35
00:03:17,495 --> 00:03:18,996
[SPEAKER_02]: But, to answer your question, self-view?

36
00:03:19,456 --> 00:03:19,676
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

37
00:03:20,417 --> 00:03:29,842
[SPEAKER_00]: So, assumption, self-view meaning you assume that things belong to you.

38
00:03:29,862 --> 00:03:31,663
[SPEAKER_00]: That's that's the fundamental assumption.

39
00:03:31,703 --> 00:03:41,929
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even when you contemplate, and not everything, by the way, it's not like Truth of Anatta is about finding out that

40
00:03:44,329 --> 00:03:52,953
[SPEAKER_00]: Things out there, like every mountain I see, every wall I see, every, I don't know, block of wood I see, I need to remind myself that it's anatta.

41
00:03:53,313 --> 00:03:53,433
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

42
00:03:53,473 --> 00:03:55,774
[SPEAKER_00]: There are things that are... that you already recognize.

43
00:03:55,794 --> 00:03:56,615
[SPEAKER_00]: This is not mine.

44
00:03:57,435 --> 00:04:05,539
[SPEAKER_00]: But the assumption is, specifically, in regard to those more personal things in your own experience.

45
00:04:06,259 --> 00:04:07,440
[SPEAKER_00]: What are those personal things?

46
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:07,740
[SPEAKER_00]: Well,

47
00:04:08,697 --> 00:04:12,219
[SPEAKER_00]: Form, feeling, perception, intention, and consciousness.

48
00:04:12,339 --> 00:04:13,500
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the assumption is.

49
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:27,668
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you will know that you're free from assumption, first of all, by... Well, by knowing what is... First of all, you find out what assumption is.

50
00:04:28,348 --> 00:04:29,208
[SPEAKER_00]: You get the right view.

51
00:04:29,469 --> 00:04:35,252
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the first... the first, eh... like, eh... milestone that you need to see.

52
00:04:35,272 --> 00:04:37,253
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is that assumption of self?

53
00:04:39,263 --> 00:04:45,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it just holding a belief, like consciously, that things are mine?

54
00:04:47,366 --> 00:04:57,910
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, perhaps we should clear up whether there's a distinction between, let's say, belief, opinions and assumptions, because assumptions is basically on the level of view, right?

55
00:04:59,602 --> 00:05:04,127
[SPEAKER_04]: So, we're not just talking about opinions and beliefs, right?

56
00:05:04,288 --> 00:05:05,609
[SPEAKER_04]: Because that you can easily change.

57
00:05:05,929 --> 00:05:07,491
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly.

58
00:05:07,611 --> 00:05:14,259
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, almost every dedicated Buddhist already has the belief that there is no self.

59
00:05:15,480 --> 00:05:17,121
[SPEAKER_03]: The belief of anath, essentially.

60
00:05:17,321 --> 00:05:18,221
[SPEAKER_03]: Ah, yeah.

61
00:05:18,501 --> 00:05:19,701
[SPEAKER_03]: That then becomes another problem.

62
00:05:19,761 --> 00:05:20,181
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

63
00:05:20,301 --> 00:05:23,362
[SPEAKER_03]: And yet, they're not free from suffering.

64
00:05:23,382 --> 00:05:27,403
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how can that... That's clearly, then, as you said, it's not on the same level.

65
00:05:27,463 --> 00:05:29,704
[SPEAKER_03]: Assumption and belief is not on the same level.

66
00:05:29,744 --> 00:05:29,984
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

67
00:05:30,024 --> 00:05:31,124
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that is generally the case.

68
00:05:31,184 --> 00:05:34,645
[SPEAKER_04]: And now, in particular, we should clear up that the Buddha actually never really taught

69
00:05:35,569 --> 00:05:39,232
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, almost to make a physical statement, there is no self.

70
00:05:39,872 --> 00:05:43,395
[SPEAKER_04]: His teaching is about recognizing how things are not self.

71
00:05:44,176 --> 00:05:49,140
[SPEAKER_04]: And that is not just like, oh, whether you add a T to the no or not, that is just splitting hairs.

72
00:05:49,160 --> 00:05:49,540
[SPEAKER_04]: No, no.

73
00:05:49,560 --> 00:05:58,247
[SPEAKER_04]: This is a very fundamental difference, because to start out by saying there is no self, is in a sense already the now of the problem.

74
00:05:58,807 --> 00:05:59,168
[SPEAKER_04]: Whereas,

75
00:06:00,728 --> 00:06:08,313
[SPEAKER_04]: Once it's clear that the whole problem is to realize that things are not self, then it is about how you are regarding things.

76
00:06:08,953 --> 00:06:09,754
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly.

77
00:06:09,934 --> 00:06:20,661
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's when people would think it's about the belief of self, like this metaphysical, almost, concept.

78
00:06:20,741 --> 00:06:22,162
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you define the self?

79
00:06:22,762 --> 00:06:23,883
[SPEAKER_03]: People would think it's about that.

80
00:06:24,823 --> 00:06:27,604
[SPEAKER_03]: But, yeah, like you said, the Buddha never discussed it in those terms.

81
00:06:27,804 --> 00:06:32,044
[SPEAKER_03]: It's actually, you have a sense of self, because you take things as yours.

82
00:06:32,404 --> 00:06:41,866
[SPEAKER_03]: Not like, first you start with the sense of self, that you have to define in such and such a way, and then, because that self exists, then that self takes things up.

83
00:06:41,966 --> 00:06:42,906
[SPEAKER_03]: It's the other way around.

84
00:06:43,106 --> 00:06:43,246
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

85
00:06:43,406 --> 00:06:53,468
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's why, whenever the Buddha would talk about not-self, he would emphasize form is not-self, feeling is not-self, perception is not-self, intention is not-self, consciousness is not-self.

86
00:06:53,508 --> 00:06:53,688
[SPEAKER_03]: So,

87
00:06:54,428 --> 00:06:57,629
[SPEAKER_03]: Anything that you would take as self would have to be one of those five things.

88
00:06:58,130 --> 00:07:07,254
[SPEAKER_03]: And so, by seeing that nothing can be self on that level, you don't need to care one bit about defining what the self is.

89
00:07:07,514 --> 00:07:09,475
[SPEAKER_03]: You will know that because you no longer take

90
00:07:10,295 --> 00:07:13,057
[SPEAKER_03]: The entirety of experience is mine, which is the five aggregates.

91
00:07:13,117 --> 00:07:14,418
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, there is no sense of self.

92
00:07:14,999 --> 00:07:17,120
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's the assumption.

93
00:07:18,141 --> 00:07:19,502
[SPEAKER_03]: That's really what the assumption is.

94
00:07:19,562 --> 00:07:21,944
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not like I have a wrong belief.

95
00:07:22,664 --> 00:07:24,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if I ask you, what is the self?

96
00:07:24,626 --> 00:07:26,367
[SPEAKER_00]: And you gave me a wrong answer or something.

97
00:07:26,747 --> 00:07:29,829
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not what the wrong view means.

98
00:07:29,890 --> 00:07:34,453
[SPEAKER_00]: It means taking, on any level,

99
00:07:34,912 --> 00:07:37,154
[SPEAKER_00]: Any aspect of your experience is mine.

100
00:07:37,955 --> 00:07:40,998
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you assume, like, if you have a...

101
00:07:42,219 --> 00:07:52,808
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know, something that... Actually, the Buddha gave a simile, like a... like a borrowed jewelry, and you may not even know that it's borrowed.

102
00:07:52,828 --> 00:07:56,251
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you assume that it's yours, and you act with it as if

103
00:07:57,132 --> 00:07:57,592
[SPEAKER_00]: It's yours.

104
00:07:57,892 --> 00:08:06,076
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, suddenly, you know, I don't know, let's say you went to a party or something, and you're wearing all the jewelry, and getting a lot of pleasure out of it.

105
00:08:06,256 --> 00:08:11,798
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, suddenly, you see the person who is the actual owner of the jewelry, and he takes it away right at that moment.

106
00:08:12,438 --> 00:08:18,460
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you... that's, practically speaking, you're assuming that that jewelry is yours.

107
00:08:18,501 --> 00:08:20,861
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's the same with the five aggregates.

108
00:08:20,921 --> 00:08:21,602
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, eh...

109
00:08:22,662 --> 00:08:41,645
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything can go well for quite a while, but the fact that the state of affairs is contrary to how you assume it to be, in this case that it does belong to you, eventually it will become clear that it's not actually the case, because...

110
00:08:43,118 --> 00:08:54,185
[SPEAKER_00]: At some point, something you don't want will be experienced, something you want will fade away, and that's how your assumption is proved wrong.

111
00:08:54,245 --> 00:08:56,907
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how would you know that there is no assumption?

112
00:08:56,947 --> 00:09:11,276
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, again, not by thinking of it in those abstract terms, like, could I get the idea about what not-self is wrong, but by asking yourself

113
00:09:12,811 --> 00:09:25,876
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there any possibility that if things change in any way, in any direction, that I'm... even that I might not expect, or it hasn't happened before, that I would suffer?

114
00:09:26,536 --> 00:09:29,037
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a measure of whether there is assumption or not.

115
00:09:29,117 --> 00:09:31,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether there is something that could make you suffer.

116
00:09:31,938 --> 00:09:39,961
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not even like... it's not like you can deceive yourself about this.

117
00:09:41,452 --> 00:09:55,957
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you have a basic sense of authenticity, as we discussed before, you need very little reflection, actually, to see that there is still suffering.

118
00:09:56,277 --> 00:09:58,018
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, that's your measure of the assumption.

119
00:09:58,598 --> 00:10:08,341
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like how right your beliefs are on a conceptual level, but how possible is it that suffering might arise for me.

120
00:10:08,381 --> 00:10:09,762
[SPEAKER_00]: And the size of your assumption

121
00:10:10,830 --> 00:10:17,532
[SPEAKER_00]: is the size of how much suffering there could be and how many things could cause it.

122
00:10:17,972 --> 00:10:27,535
[SPEAKER_00]: So, on account of reducing the size of that assumption, the size of the suffering would also decrease.

123
00:10:27,555 --> 00:10:32,057
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's like the noble disciples.

124
00:10:32,117 --> 00:10:35,138
[SPEAKER_00]: They still have some degree of assumption, but it can never be

125
00:10:36,662 --> 00:10:38,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Nearly the same amount as a pūtugina.

126
00:10:38,383 --> 00:10:44,425
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why, as the Buddha said, it's like seven grains of sand for a sotāpanna.

127
00:10:45,305 --> 00:10:52,708
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the size of the assumption compared to the size of the pūtugina's assumption, the assumption itself.

128
00:10:52,808 --> 00:10:55,809
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, seven grains of sand compared to the whole Himalayas.

129
00:10:55,849 --> 00:10:56,329
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah.

130
00:10:57,489 --> 00:10:58,269
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's the...

131
00:10:59,010 --> 00:11:01,170
[SPEAKER_03]: If there is suffering, there has...

132
00:11:01,190 --> 00:11:03,091
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry, if there is assumption, there has to be suffering.

133
00:11:03,671 --> 00:11:05,272
[SPEAKER_00]: If there is suffering, there has to be assumption.

134
00:11:06,652 --> 00:11:08,333
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's really your measure.

135
00:11:08,393 --> 00:11:21,300
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, if you have developed that criteria of authenticity that we discussed before, then you know for sure how to investigate your liability.

136
00:11:22,680 --> 00:11:26,562
[SPEAKER_00]: You know how to see whether you're liable to something or not.

137
00:11:27,723 --> 00:11:32,225
[SPEAKER_00]: And, yeah, that's how asking yourself

138
00:11:34,345 --> 00:11:35,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Death, for example.

139
00:11:36,046 --> 00:11:36,466
[SPEAKER_00]: Sickness.

140
00:11:36,766 --> 00:11:37,086
[SPEAKER_00]: Aging.

141
00:11:38,206 --> 00:11:52,710
[SPEAKER_00]: People usually think of the practice as some sort of... these mystical truths that you discover that are very pleasant to find out how your consciousness is constantly changing every second or something.

142
00:11:53,451 --> 00:11:56,892
[SPEAKER_00]: But the dhamma starts with the Buddha himself.

143
00:11:56,932 --> 00:11:57,532
[SPEAKER_00]: He went forth

144
00:11:58,132 --> 00:12:01,714
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he wanted to find a way out of sickness, aging and death.

145
00:12:02,855 --> 00:12:05,696
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because he wanted to find out these very...

146
00:12:05,776 --> 00:12:07,257
[SPEAKER_04]: The ultimate tools of the universe.

147
00:12:07,277 --> 00:12:08,058
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

148
00:12:08,518 --> 00:12:12,740
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that is... that is the... everything that he taught.

149
00:12:12,841 --> 00:12:18,644
[SPEAKER_00]: Assumption, ignorance, all of that pertains to that liability to sickness, aging and death.

150
00:12:19,684 --> 00:12:24,988
[SPEAKER_00]: So, any person who, even a sotāpanna or anāgāmī even,

151
00:12:26,008 --> 00:12:35,494
[SPEAKER_00]: If they reflect properly, they would be able to see, just by thinking, by reflecting, oh, are there things that could cause suffering in this mind?

152
00:12:35,614 --> 00:12:36,895
[SPEAKER_00]: And the answer will have to be yes.

153
00:12:38,616 --> 00:12:46,300
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's really about, like, developing that self-transparency, so that your questioning of your liability to suffering is accurate.

154
00:12:46,900 --> 00:12:51,123
[SPEAKER_00]: And based on that, you indirectly know, okay, there is assumption left.

155
00:12:56,091 --> 00:13:04,238
[SPEAKER_04]: Now, ordinary to you would be, I exist and then, because I exist, I have desire for this and I regard things like that.

156
00:13:05,319 --> 00:13:08,622
[SPEAKER_04]: But you just said that actually assumption of self is secondary.

157
00:13:08,662 --> 00:13:11,044
[SPEAKER_04]: It is the product of taking things as mine.

158
00:13:12,105 --> 00:13:16,469
[SPEAKER_04]: So, in order to uproot that assumption, can we explore in more detail

159
00:13:18,255 --> 00:13:20,378
[SPEAKER_04]: What is involved in regarding things as mine?

160
00:13:21,138 --> 00:13:26,624
[SPEAKER_04]: Because when that becomes more clear, the result will be that there is no longer room for assumption of self.

161
00:13:27,946 --> 00:13:31,029
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what is then practically involved in regarding things as mine?

162
00:13:31,549 --> 00:13:32,230
[SPEAKER_04]: What does that mean?

163
00:13:37,553 --> 00:13:49,079
[SPEAKER_02]: Attending rightly things, so not recognizing or discerning the womb of things, or attending without yonisona and sikkhāra.

164
00:13:50,300 --> 00:13:59,644
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in this way, you basically take the experience, beginning of the experience, from the point of self, yeah?

165
00:14:00,045 --> 00:14:06,388
[SPEAKER_02]: First is, I am the first one, and I'm attending to anything out there,

166
00:14:08,532 --> 00:14:11,594
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that would be the wrong type of attention.

167
00:14:11,834 --> 00:14:15,535
[SPEAKER_02]: Is there something more concrete, even before that?

168
00:14:17,496 --> 00:14:21,218
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, in terms of... you mean like precepts?

169
00:14:21,318 --> 00:14:21,538
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

170
00:14:22,119 --> 00:14:22,299
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

171
00:14:22,319 --> 00:14:22,959
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yeah.

172
00:14:23,980 --> 00:14:30,803
[SPEAKER_02]: You'd have to basically prepare the ground for it, so to speak, by keeping the...

173
00:14:33,444 --> 00:14:48,892
[SPEAKER_04]: So, is it by acting out of pressure that one is practically regarding things as mine?

174
00:14:49,452 --> 00:14:55,675
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, can you act out of pressure without you already regarding things as mine?

175
00:15:01,505 --> 00:15:09,233
[SPEAKER_04]: Or, does every acting out of pressure necessarily implies that you take things to be yours?

176
00:15:10,875 --> 00:15:20,444
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's even like pressure, and you can sort of particularize it into passion, aversion, and destruction.

177
00:15:20,464 --> 00:15:21,645
[SPEAKER_01]: The acting out of pressure.

178
00:15:21,726 --> 00:15:24,689
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you know that if you have such actions,

179
00:15:26,500 --> 00:15:33,446
[SPEAKER_01]: With no regard whatsoever for your abstract ideas about not-self, you have a sense of self.

180
00:15:34,347 --> 00:15:36,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, by committing those actions.

181
00:15:36,969 --> 00:15:40,171
[SPEAKER_01]: So, actually, that's an important thing I forgot to bring up.

182
00:15:40,732 --> 00:15:50,680
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha said, in one sutta, he talked about 11 things that, if you don't understand, it's impossible for you to make progress in the dhamma.

183
00:15:51,340 --> 00:15:52,201
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the...

184
00:15:52,441 --> 00:15:52,882
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it's...

185
00:15:55,008 --> 00:16:09,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Madhyaminikaya 33 or 34, he said, one of them was, a bhikkhu does not understand, a fool is determined by his actions, and the wise person is determined by their actions.

186
00:16:10,257 --> 00:16:17,241
